h1: "Pakistan Salary Tax Calculator 2026-27"
extraCss: "/tools/pakistan-salary-tax-calculator/style.css"
quickAnswer: "In tax year 2026-27, a salaried person earning Rs 100,000 a month (Rs 1,200,000 a year) pays Rs 6,000 a year in income tax, which is Rs 500 a month. Salary up to Rs 600,000 a year is tax-free; above that, tax is a fixed amount for your slab plus 1% to 35% of the income above the slab's starting point."
formula:
  title: "Salary Tax Formula"
  expression: "Annual tax = Fixed amount of your slab + Slab rate × (Annual taxable income − Slab starting point)"
  explanation: "Find the slab your annual taxable salary falls in, take that slab's fixed amount, and add the slab rate applied only to the part of your income above the slab's starting point. Divide the annual tax by 12 for the monthly deduction. In 2025-26 a 9% surcharge on the tax applied when taxable income exceeded Rs 10,000,000; it does not apply to salaried individuals in 2026-27."
  variables:
    - name: "Annual taxable income"
      description: "Monthly taxable salary × 12, or your annual taxable salary."
    - name: "Fixed amount"
      description: "The total tax due on all the income below your slab, already worked out for you in the slab table."
    - name: "Slab rate"
      description: "The marginal rate, which applies only to income above the slab's starting point."
workedExamples:
  - title: "Monthly salary Rs 100,000"
    inputs:
      - "Monthly salary: Rs 100,000"
      - "Annual taxable income: Rs 1,200,000"
      - "Slab: Rs 600,001 to Rs 1,200,000 (1%)"
    steps:
      - "Income above the slab start: 1,200,000 − 600,000 = 600,000"
      - "Annual tax: 0 + 1% × 600,000 = Rs 6,000"
      - "Monthly tax: 6,000 ÷ 12 = Rs 500"
      - "Monthly take-home: 100,000 − 500 = Rs 99,500"
    result: "Rs 500 tax a month, Rs 99,500 take-home (effective rate 0.5%). The same as in 2025-26."
    link: "/tools/pakistan-salary-tax-calculator/?salary=100000&period=monthly&year=2026-27#tool"
  - title: "Monthly salary Rs 250,000"
    inputs:
      - "Monthly salary: Rs 250,000"
      - "Annual taxable income: Rs 3,000,000"
      - "Slab: Rs 2,200,001 to Rs 3,200,000 (Rs 116,000 + 20%)"
    steps:
      - "Income above the slab start: 3,000,000 − 2,200,000 = 800,000"
      - "Annual tax: 116,000 + 20% × 800,000 = Rs 276,000"
      - "Monthly tax: 276,000 ÷ 12 = Rs 23,000"
      - "Monthly take-home: 250,000 − 23,000 = Rs 227,000"
      - "2025-26 for comparison: 116,000 + 23% × 800,000 = Rs 300,000 a year, Rs 25,000 a month"
    result: "Rs 23,000 tax a month, Rs 227,000 take-home (effective rate 9.2%). You save Rs 2,000 a month compared to 2025-26."
    link: "/tools/pakistan-salary-tax-calculator/?salary=250000&period=monthly&year=2026-27#tool"
  - title: "Monthly salary Rs 500,000"
    inputs:
      - "Monthly salary: Rs 500,000"
      - "Annual taxable income: Rs 6,000,000"
      - "Slab: Rs 5,600,001 to Rs 7,000,000 (Rs 976,000 + 32%)"
    steps:
      - "Income above the slab start: 6,000,000 − 5,600,000 = 400,000"
      - "Annual tax: 976,000 + 32% × 400,000 = Rs 1,104,000"
      - "Monthly tax: 1,104,000 ÷ 12 = Rs 92,000"
      - "Monthly take-home: 500,000 − 92,000 = Rs 408,000"
      - "2025-26 for comparison: 616,000 + 35% × 1,900,000 = Rs 1,281,000 a year, Rs 106,750 a month"
    result: "Rs 92,000 tax a month, Rs 408,000 take-home (effective rate 18.4%). You save Rs 14,750 a month compared to 2025-26."
    link: "/tools/pakistan-salary-tax-calculator/?salary=500000&period=monthly&year=2026-27#tool"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Applying the top rate to the whole salary"
    description: "Falling in the 32% slab does not mean 32% of your salary goes in tax. The rate applies only to income above the slab's starting point; the income below it is covered by the fixed amount. That is why Rs 6,000,000 a year is taxed at an effective 18.4%, not 32%."
  - title: "Forgetting the fixed amount"
    description: "Multiplying only the excess by the slab rate leaves out the tax on everything below the slab. For Rs 3,000,000 a year, 20% × 800,000 is Rs 160,000, but the correct tax is Rs 116,000 + Rs 160,000 = Rs 276,000."
  - title: "Mixing monthly and annual figures"
    description: "The slabs are annual. Comparing a monthly salary of Rs 250,000 directly with a Rs 600,000 threshold, or dividing an annual fixed amount by 12 in only one place, gives badly wrong answers. Convert the salary to an annual figure first, work out the annual tax, then divide by 12."
  - title: "Using the wrong tax year"
    description: "Pakistan's tax year runs from July to June. Salary paid from July 2026 to June 2027 falls under tax year 2026-27; salary paid before July 2026 uses the 2025-26 slabs."
  - title: "Treating gross pay as taxable income"
    description: "Taxable income can differ from the salary on your offer letter because of exempt allowances, deductions and tax credits. This calculator applies the slabs to the amount you enter, so enter your taxable salary for the closest estimate."
faqs:
  - q: "How much tax will I pay on a salary of Rs 100,000 per month in 2026-27?"
    a: "Rs 500 a month. Rs 100,000 a month is Rs 1,200,000 a year, which falls in the 1% slab: 1% of the Rs 600,000 above the Rs 600,000 threshold is Rs 6,000 a year, or Rs 500 a month. Your take-home pay is Rs 99,500."
  - q: "What are the new income tax slabs for salaried persons in 2026-27?"
    a: "Annual salary up to Rs 600,000 is tax-free. Above that the rates are 1% (up to Rs 1,200,000), 11% (up to Rs 2,200,000), 20% (up to Rs 3,200,000), 25% (up to Rs 4,100,000), 29% (up to Rs 5,600,000), 32% (up to Rs 7,000,000) and 35% above Rs 7,000,000, each on top of the fixed amount shown in the slab table."
  - q: "Is salary up to Rs 50,000 per month tax-free in Pakistan?"
    a: "Yes. Rs 50,000 a month is Rs 600,000 a year, which is the top of the 0% slab in both 2025-26 and 2026-27, so no income tax is due on taxable salary up to that amount."
  - q: "How is monthly income tax on salary calculated in Pakistan?"
    a: "Multiply your monthly taxable salary by 12, find your slab, and work out the annual tax as the slab's fixed amount plus the slab rate on the income above the slab's starting point. Divide the annual tax by 12 to get the monthly deduction."
  - q: "What changed in salary tax from 2025-26 to 2026-27?"
    a: "Rates were cut in four slabs (23% to 20%, 30% to 25%, and 35% to 29% and 32% in two new slabs between Rs 4,100,000 and Rs 7,000,000), the top 35% rate now starts at Rs 7,000,000 instead of Rs 4,100,000, and the 9% surcharge on salaried income above Rs 10,000,000 was removed."
  - q: "Is there a surcharge on salary income in 2026-27?"
    a: "No. The 9% surcharge on the tax of salaried individuals earning more than Rs 10,000,000 a year applied in 2025-26 and does not apply in 2026-27."
  - q: "What is the difference between my tax slab and my effective tax rate?"
    a: "Your slab rate is the rate on your last rupees of income. Your effective rate is your total tax divided by your total income, which is always lower because the income in the lower slabs is taxed at lower rates. Rs 3,000,000 a year is in the 20% slab but has an effective rate of 9.2%."
  - q: "Does this calculator include allowances, exemptions or tax credits?"
    a: "No. It applies the salaried slabs to the taxable salary you enter. It does not account for exempt allowances, deductions, tax credits or rebates, so treat the result as an estimate and confirm your liability with FBR or a tax adviser."
