    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="how-calculated-heading">
      <h2 id="how-calculated-heading">How salary tax is calculated in Pakistan</h2>
      <p>Income tax on a salary in Pakistan is worked out on a yearly figure, even though most employers deduct it every month. Three ideas are enough to follow the whole calculation.</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Taxable income:</strong> the part of your annual salary that is subject to tax. For most salaried people this is close to their annual pay, but exempt allowances, deductions and tax credits can make it lower. This calculator applies the slabs to whatever amount you enter.</li>
        <li><strong>Slabs:</strong> the law divides annual income into bands. The first Rs 600,000 a year is taxed at 0%, and each band above it has its own rate, rising to 35% on income above Rs 7,000,000 in 2026-27.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed amount plus marginal rate:</strong> each slab lists a fixed amount and a rate. The fixed amount is simply the tax on all the income in the slabs below yours, already added up. The rate, called the marginal rate, applies only to the income above your slab's starting point.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>So moving into a higher slab never makes your take-home pay fall: only the rupees above the new threshold are taxed at the higher rate. Your effective tax rate, which is total tax divided by total income, is always lower than your slab rate. Your employer deducts roughly one-twelfth of the annual tax from each month's pay, which is the monthly tax figure the calculator shows.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="budget-changes-heading">
      <h2 id="budget-changes-heading">What changed in Budget 2026-27</h2>
      <p>The Finance Act 2026 changed the salaried slabs for the tax year that runs from July 2026 to June 2027. Income up to Rs 2,200,000 a year is taxed exactly as before, so the changes matter most to middle and higher earners.</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Rates cut in four slabs:</strong> income from Rs 2,200,001 to Rs 3,200,000 is now taxed at 20% instead of 23%; Rs 3,200,001 to Rs 4,100,000 at 25% instead of 30%; Rs 4,100,001 to Rs 5,600,000 at 29% instead of 35%; and Rs 5,600,001 to Rs 7,000,000 at 32% instead of 35%.</li>
        <li><strong>Two new slabs:</strong> the old single top band above Rs 4,100,000 is now split into three, so the top 35% rate starts at Rs 7,000,000 a year instead of Rs 4,100,000. That takes the table from six slabs to eight.</li>
        <li><strong>Surcharge removed:</strong> in 2025-26, salaried individuals with taxable income above Rs 10,000,000 paid an extra 9% of their tax as a surcharge. There is no surcharge for salaried individuals in 2026-27.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Because each slab's fixed amount carries forward the lower rates, everyone above Rs 2,200,000 a year pays less tax in 2026-27 than in 2025-26 on the same salary. Choose 2025-26 in the calculator, or read the comparison line under the result, to see the difference for your salary in rupees per month.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="estimate-heading">
      <h2 id="estimate-heading">What this calculator does not include</h2>
      <p>This is an estimate for salaried individuals only. It applies the salaried slabs to the taxable salary you enter and does not account for allowances, exemptions, deductions, tax credits or rebates, or for income from business, property or investments, which is taxed under different rules. Rates for non-salaried individuals are also different. If your situation includes any of these, confirm your tax with FBR or a qualified tax adviser before relying on the figure.</p>
    </section>
